    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Fasilides chose Gondar as his royal camp in 1636, and it became the first permanent capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a>, where he, Yohannes I and Iyasu I built castles and churches.</li><li>After Iyasu I died in 1706 the city declined as Tigrayan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a> power grew, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras">Ras</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikael-sehul">Mikael Sehul</a> seized it in 1767 at the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zemene-mesafint">Zemene Mesafint</a>.</li><li>Tewodros ordered it sacked in 1864 and the Mahdists attacked it in 1888 and 1889, leaving a town of ruins; the Italians occupied it in 1936, and it later became a provincial town.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Charles Poncet, the French physician of Iyasu I, was the first European to describe Gondar, in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> was generally thought to have no fixed capital.</p></aside>
    </section>

