    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1636</span><span class="rf-text">Emperor Fasilides selects Gondar as his royal camp and begins its castle</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1669</span><span class="rf-text">Emperor Yohannes I orders Muslims and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> to form their own sectors in the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1706</span><span class="rf-text">Iyasu I dies and the city begins to decline</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1767</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras">Ras</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikael-sehul">Mikael Sehul</a> seizes Gondar, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zemene-mesafint">Zemene Mesafint</a> follows</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1864</span><span class="rf-text">Tewodros orders the city sacked and his soldiers plunder its clergy and monasteries</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1888</span><span class="rf-text">Mahdist forces enter Gondar and burn its churches</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1936</span><span class="rf-text">Italian troops occupy Gondar without a fight</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1991</span><span class="rf-text">Forces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-peoples-revolutionary-democratic-front">EPRDF</a> occupy Gondar in May</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/amda-seyon-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amda-seyon-i.jpg" alt="Amda Seyon I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/amda-seyon-i">Amda Seyon I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amda_Seyon1.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Gondar lies in the North Gondar Zone of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amhara">Amhara</a> Region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-tana">Lake Tana</a> on the Lesser Angereb River and southwest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semien">Simien</a> Mountains. The name Gondar was first mentioned in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amda-seyon-i">Amda Seyon I</a> as the name of a regiment of soldiers, probably stationed in Wegera, which guarded nearby trade routes and controlled a restive population.</p><p>In 1636 Emperor Fasilides (r. 1632 to 1667) selected Gondar as his katama, or royal camp. The site was about 35 km due north of Lake Tana in the midst of Kémant country, and he moved there from Danqaz. Caravans from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> converged there, which brought trading benefits, and Lent and the rainy season, when expeditions were prohibited, kept the emperor at the capital. Gondar became the first permanent capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a>, set among the fertile lands of Dambia and Wegera. Fasilides ordered the building of the Fasil Ghebbi, or Fasilides Castle, and established five other churches, among them Mädòane Aläm and Fit Abbo. A pleasure pool with a stone pavilion beside the Qaha river became known as the Bath of Fasilides.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1648 a Yemeni ambassador, Hassan ibn Ahmad al-Haymi, visited the palace and described it as a great house of stone and lime. The head stonemason was an Indian named Abdal Kerim, who had worked on the palace of Emperor Susenyos I at Danqaz. In the castle al-Haymi saw beds from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, mattresses from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and courtiers in gowns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> brocade embroidered with gold.</p><p>When Fasilides died in 1667, Gondar was so well established that his successor Yohannes I (1667 to 1682) did not seek a new capital, as earlier monarchs had, and spent half of each year in the city. Yohannes built a large number of edifices, among them a two-storey library and a chancery with a tower. Iyasu I (1682 to 1706) built a new castle and several other churches, and during his reign the city's population was estimated to have exceeded 60,000. Caravans from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> converged on and dispersed from the city, and its long-distance trade lasted until the middle of the 19th century.</p><p>Gondar prospered until the reign of Tekle Giyorgis (1779 to 1784), nicknamed Fäsame Mängest, "the End of Government". Bahru Zewde noted that French travellers called it "the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> of Abyssinia".</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mikael-sehul"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mikael-sehul.jpg" alt="Mikael Sehul" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mikael-sehul">Mikael Sehul</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ras_Mikael_Sehul_burst_coin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">James Bruce, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Fasilides, Yohannes I and Iyasu I ruled from Gondar in turn, and the city was the seat of the empire's emperors through the 18th century. After the death of Iyasu I in 1706 it began to decline, as most emperors preferred a life of luxury to politics. Tigray gained importance, since the tolls on caravans bound for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> could be levied by whoever controlled it, and the Tigrayan warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras">Ras</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikael-sehul">Mikael Sehul</a> rose. Empress Mentewab and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amhara">Amhara</a> lords of her family held power, and she was regent for her grandson.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iyasu-ii">Iyasu II</a> died on 27 June 1755, and Mikael hurried to the capital, where he met the child <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iyoas-i">Iyoas I</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a> influence in the city grew, and it was guarded by 3,000 Oromo troops. Iyoas appealed to Mikael, who became master of Gondar and seized it in 1767. Mikael killed Iyoas and crowned the 70-year-old Yohannes II, and the empire entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zemene-mesafint">Zemene Mesafint</a>, an era of declining central authority.</p><p>In 1841 Wube of Tigre seized the town from Ras Ali II. In 1854 Kassa Hailu took control, and the city assembly proclaimed him King of Kings; as Tewodros he was little drawn to Gondar. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yohannes-iv">Yohannes IV</a> spent the early part of his reign in Debre Tabor, and the court left the city. Italian troops occupied it on 1 April 1936, and after fighting between British and Italian forces on 13 to 17 November 1941 the Italians withdrew. On 23 February 1991 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-peoples-revolutionary-democratic-front">EPRDF</a> began an offensive against the government in Gondar and occupied it in May.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1669 to 1670 Yohannes I ordered the Muslims and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> to separate from the Christians and form sectors of their own. By 1676 Muslims had settled in the lower town near the Qaha, in a quarter called Eslam Bet or Eslamge, and the Beta Israel were given four quarters, among them Abarra and Kayla Meda. The royal enclosure was known as Makkabäbiya or Fail Gémb. Addebabay was a public square and market, Abun Bet the home of the abun, Qañn Bet the noble quarter, and Déngay the quarter of Christian merchants. Yohannes also ordered that people lending money should not charge interest.</p><p>Gondar was a noted centre of the ecclesiastical learning of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-orthodox-church">Ethiopian Orthodox</a> Tewahedo Church and had 44 churches, for many years more than any other settlement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. The city and its surrounding countryside are the homeland of most Ethiopian Jews. In the early 1900s the city traded in salt from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afar">Afar</a> lowlands, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, mules and goats from Begemder, honey and wax from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semien">Semien</a> and Wegera, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and gold from Bani Shangul, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Kaffa</a>. Italian merchants and a doctor lived there.</p><p>In 1994 the largest groups in the city were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amhara">Amhara</a> (88.91%), the Tigrayans (6.74%) and the Qemant (2.37%). <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amharic">Amharic</a> was the first language of 94.57%, and 83.31% were Ethiopian Orthodox Christians and 15.83% Muslims.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1770 Wolda Hawariat claimed that Gondar had an epidemic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a>. In the reign of Emperor Gigar (1821 to 1830) soldiers camped in the market, plundered the Déngay district and ignored the emperor and the Ečagē, the chief of customs. The missionary Samuel Gobat, who lived in Gondar in 1830, described frequent disturbances in which residents fled to churches. In 1832 to 1833 the soldiers of Ali II plundered homes and seized supplies, and civilians hid their goods in churches. Merchants could not bring goods in, and scholars lamented the collapse of schools.</p><p>In 1864 Tewodros ordered the Muslim inhabitants to convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> or leave, and many fled the country. By the end of that year, having declared Gondar a "town of priests who do not love me", he ordered it violently sacked. His soldiers robbed the priests, burned their residences and plundered the monasteries.</p><p>With the departure of the court, the population, well over 60,000 in the 18th century, was said by some observers to have fallen to 8,000 in the 1870s. According to the historian Heruy Wald Selassie, the Mahdists entered the city in January 1888 and burnt all the churches, killed those who resisted and enslaved women and children. They returned in June 1889 and massacred men and women alike. By the end of the century it was little more than a ghost town in ruins.</p><p>Under Italian occupation about 2,000 Europeans lived there within two years. By the 1950s the population had fallen to 13,000, with few churches remaining. A severe drought hit Gondar in the 1970s, worst in 1983 to 1986.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The most famous buildings of Gondar stand in the royal enclosure, Fasil Ghebbi, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>, which has been called the "Camelot of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>". They include the castle of Fasilides, the palace of Iyasu, Dawit's Hall, a banqueting hall, stables, the castle of Empress Mentewab, a chancellery, a library and three churches. Near the city lie Fasilides' Bath, which is blessed and then opened for bathing in an annual ceremony, the Qusquam complex built by Mentewab, the palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras">Ras</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikael-sehul">Mikael Sehul</a> and the church of Debre Berhan Selassie.</p><p>The Italian occupation of the late 1930s left its mark on the centre. Colonial planners laid out a piazza beside the castle compound and new buildings in a modernist style, and built roads to Azezo, Gojjam and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>. They also built the city's first mosque with a minaret beside the Saturday market. The main piazza still has shops, a cinema and public buildings in a simplified Italian Moderne style.</p><p>Gondar is today a city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amhara">Amhara</a> Region with an estimated population of about 443,000. It was also the capital of the former Begemder Province. The University of Gondar, which includes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>'s main faculty of medicine, is in the city.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Gondar, in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, was chosen as a royal camp by Fasilides in 1636 and became the first permanent capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a>, with castles and churches. It declined after 1706, was sacked in 1864 and raided by the Mahdists, and was occupied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1936.</em></p>

